es  we're also  trying to dissect the mechanism of  action of aspirin  within more relevant and vivo models  and  one of the concepts is that aspirin may  have an impact on lymph angiogenesis  and so one of the kind of key goals  within this work package is to establish  robust animal models combined with  imaging approaches to look at the impact  of aspirin on lymphangiogenesis  and again as i mentioned this particular  work package  is focused on both aspirants and statins  and we have some data from our lab  showing that  when we look at the expression of the  one of the drug targets for statins hmg  choreo reductase at a protein expression  level within breast tumors  high expression of that particular  enzyme  this drug target for staffing seems to  be associated with a  modified response to endocrine therapy  and so we're very interested in really  seeing if this is  really relevant this observation we've  seen in breast cancer tissues is  relevant in the population-based level  and that's an area that we're actually  exploring  within  the central model  work package three is looking at the  impact of our or looking at the  evolution of tumors  during therapy  uh particularly in the context of  endocrine therapy and  therapies which target her2 protein  this particular activity is led by a  professor brian hennessey  who's a medical oncologist based in the  royal college of surgeons in ireland and  the deputy lead is professor dex  higgins who  is a kind of world famous  bioinformatician who developed for  example the sequels alignment program  cluster  so in a variety of different  kind of concept studies  what we're doing is leveraging the  resources that we have within the this  icork our clinical trials infrastructure  within ireland  where we can actually look at patients  in a longitudinal manner looking at the  how they evolve during treatment at a  molecular level  and so what we've done is we've  identified a number of different  clinical trials within the portfolio of  icorg where we have patient samples at  various different intervals during  treatment for example before  before new adjuvant treatment at the  point of surgery  at point of relapse etc  and what we're doing is we're getting  we've gotten access to those materials  and we're performing multi-pronged ohmic  assessment for example looking at  whole exome changes at a mutational  level within the dna looking at  transphobic alterations using rna  sequencing and then also looking at  protein expression and signaling changes  using reverse phase protein ray  technology  and again we have a lot of expertise in  really kind of marrying these two are  these various different types of data  sets together  with a dna alterations rna alterations  or protein expression alterations  and we want to really  come up with really i suppose novel  pathway alterations which  maybe explain for example either  preferential sensitivity to these types  of treatments or resistance or emerge  emerging resistance  so following these discovery and  studies which are still ongoing we also  have a program of downstream extensive  validation where we're going to look at  really extrapolating these findings and  a much broader set of patient samples  really to bring these observations  closer to a clinical implementation  work package 4 is really focused on this  concept of combining novel agents  together  to really come up with  novel therapy combinations  based on a more sophisticated  understanding of the biology of breast  cancer rather than just some empirical  mishmash of combinations or drugs that  you might use  this activity is led by a noted medical  oncologist in ireland professor john  crown  who's based in university college dublin  and dublin city university as well as  saint vincent's university hospital and  the deputy lead is professor water  culture director of the conway institute  and also systems biology ireland  so this particular  work package has three main components  which i'm going to outline here the  first is really to develop and utilize a  computational modeling framework  where we can actually link this ohmic  data for example data coming from this  work package tree  which i previously mentioned but also  external data that's already out there  there's a slew of  ohmic data and other similar data set  out there in the breast cancer context  that we can actually leverage  with  various different signal transduction  models which actually been developed  within the context of systems biology  ireland  and the idea is really to use this  information to really come up at an  silicon level with novel druggable  nodes which can then be subsequently  tested  experimentally within the lab using in  vitro and and then following that in  vivo models using  uh quote gene knock-down technologies  but also  therapeutic agents  alongside this computation modeling  framework we also have a plan we have  ongoing activities to really perform  robust functional screening  where we use various different  combinations of drugs and  libraries of si rnas or short terpene  nuclear rna to knock down genes of  interest  in essentially synthetic lethality  screens in various different model  systems to come up with novel  combinations  so we have both a modeling concept and  also a functional screening  aspect  so along these more kind of generic  broad-based approaches we have some very  targeted projects where we're actually  going with already kind of known  uh druggable entities and looking at  specific combinations  we're also looking at targeting mutant  53 using specific agents which can  actually  change the properties of mutant 53  converted into more wall type form and  then also we have a specific basic  research program program looking at  epigenetic silencing of  the p16 gene  so the last work package is looking at  kind of diagnostic implications  this work is led by professor joachim  pran who's based in the college of  surgeons  and also the deputy lead is professor  lorena driscoll who's a kind of leading  investigator emerging investigator  within trinity college dublin  and so this program has four different  aspects  the first actually is looking at um  apoptotic signatures so professors  friends group has actually spent a lot  of work really modeling various  different components within the  apoptosis signaling pathway  using a systems modeling approach and  he's shown in a colorectal cancer  context by by measuring essentially a  small number of components within the  apoptosis signaling pathway  he can actually come up with a his group  can actually come up with a predictor of  inherent  human sensitivity or chemo resistance so  he's shown this concept  uh using clinical tissues from  colorectal cancer patients and one of  the guilds behind the center is really  to validate a similar concept or a  similar approach within breast cancer  professor o'driscoll has put down a lot  of work really on looking at circulating  nucleic acids particularly micrornas but  also more conventional messenger rnas  within cancer patients this work has  also been done by professor uh karen and  uh dr wilson with warren galway  and there's a big interest in really  using these types of circulating nucleic  acids either free circulating nucleic  acids or ones which which are for  example bound within exosomes as  biomarkers of response to treatment  professor leonie young in the college of  surgeons has a huge wealth of expertise  in looking at molecular determinants of  response to endocrine therapy  and she has a particular problem where  she's identified a very interesting  putative mark of endocrine resistance  that she does she she is bringing  forward at a further level of technical  validation within the center  and the last program here within this  work package is led by professor  rosemarie o'connor in university college  cork  and she has really a huge amount of  expertise in ig